  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126074,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients typically present with hypopigmented papules arranged in plaques.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:29:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126075,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>Tumour of the follicular infundibulum is composed of anastomosing horizontally arranged cords of pale epithelial cells, connecting at intervals with the epidermis and creating a fenestrated pattern {6392372}. Most cells within the cords are large, with pale cytoplasm (as a result of abundant intracytoplasmic glycogen) and a small round or oval nucleus. Peripheral cells are basaloid, with basophilic, poorly defined cytoplasm and palisading of the nuclei. At the periphery of neoplastic cords, epithelial prominences consisting of small infundibular cysts are seen. Small ductal structures with irregular luminal borders, similar to sebaceous ducts, and small clusters of mature sebocytes may be also seen {11401679; 21747619}. The stroma of is composed of loose fibrovascular connective tissue with numerous elastic fibres arranged in a reticular pattern.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Superficial basal cell carcinoma shows peripheral clefting and cytological atypia.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:30:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
